<!--Post: 4176--><!--Time: 1105824734-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4176"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Researcher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Researcher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 1:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My opinion is - there will not be a drop on the 15th, 16th, or ever again. BTK will sink back into the shadows and not bring any more attention to himself. A years worth of clues - the details of which are kept from us except for a few dribs and drabs - have done little to bring him and the authorities any closer together for capture. <BR> <BR>The WPD blew it big time right from the beginning. The Nancy Fox audio released to the public for &#34;help&#34; years after it was recorded. Same thing with the &#34;Oh Anna&#34; poem. <BR>Communications from letters in library books, post office drops, packages to KAKE etc... All nada. So what are we left with? Really very little. Even the puzzle/cryptograms so many of us have been working on comes from a poor screen capture from television. <BR> <BR>However - from an informational view we were VERY lucky in December. Do we know how lucky we were? Had the person who found the package in Murdock Park called the WPD instead of the media we would be sitting in the the dark about it&#39;s contents. As it is now all that we know is that it contained the driver&#39;s license of Nancy Fox. The other material/items it contained have not been commented on. <BR> <BR>It seems to me that the tight cloak of secrecy LE uses to protect the case and the investigation is overkill. So much could have been released - even in redacted form - like photos of the communications - something/anything that might trigger a memory. Of course, along with that, comes the hundreds of tips that further complicate and extend the time-line of the investigation. But that is the nature of the beast. You go public when you hit the brick wall. You request assistance when you are spinning your wheels and getting nowhere. <BR> <BR>The info &#40;if true&#41; re: 1939, Petra,. trains,. ham radio, fiddle playing grandfather etc... how old was that information? Was it contained in a communication from 2004? <BR> <BR>The Nancy Fox necklace - was that in a communication from this year, or old information released by the police as a result of the Murdock Park drop? <BR> <BR>From an amateur sleuth perspective we want to know all the information. It satisfies our curiosity, may reduce endless speculation, but more importantly with the brain power here and elswhere, and the investigative skills held by some, it just might be that one, or two may pick up on a very significant clue that could connect another dot. <BR> <BR>I am not saying that an Internet community will catch the BTK killer, but that the right information - if released - to the public could go a long way in tightening the rope on BTK. Apparently the WPD &#40;and also on the advice of the feds&#41; are doing what they believe is sufficient as far as public information access. I say are, and have been wrong. But then again, they are the pros. <BR> <BR>The feds have stepped into the action, and federal funds have been made available to assist the WPD. I don&#39;t think it is a task-force, or a large block of manpower that will catch BTK, nor can his capture be bought by throwing federal funding into the investigation. <BR> <BR>My final thought is that, although most are pleased that the WPD has released a few clues here and there, what they have done in that regard is much too little and way too late. Now we are grasping at straws in the wind. <BR> <BR>As for BTK, he will probably end out his retirement days by observing from afar the circus of the investigation that - with his once again self imposed silence - will, after a period of time, once again wind down. All that will be left are a few books, lots of theories and speculations, Internet chat rooms. All of the important evidence will still be locked away so as not to jeopardize a case that will never be prosecuted, and a new group of LE people will replace the old guard. <BR> <BR>BTK will go down in history like his friend the Zodiac. He will become a cottage industry - and will forever remain unknown. <BR> <BR>Would you believe it? I am by nature an optimistic guy<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4176-->
<!--Post: 4183--><!--Time: 1105842850-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4183"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 6:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;m still giving BTK some time. Unless he&#39;s very seriously ill -- the December park drop suggests BTK may not be dying or even sick, but that&#39;s not conclusive -- I think he&#39;ll be heard from again. That said, I agree almost word for word what you&#39;ve posted. <BR> <BR>One thing. The opening part of the strange, patchwork &#34;list of information&#34; released 11/30. <BR><i>&#34;Since last March, BTK has sent numerous communications to the media and to the police. In these letters he has provided certain background information about himself which <b>he claims</b> is accurate. Based upon a review of that information, the following <b>facts</b> about BTK are being made available to the public in hopes of identifying BTK.&#34;</i> &#40;emphasis supplied.&#41; In reading this carefully, it seems WPD is taking BTK&#39;s word as fact. What BTK &#34;claims&#34; turns into &#34;facts&#34; twelve words later. I wonder if BTK demanded that this be released? It wouldn&#39;t surprise me if he had.  I give BTK some time but it looks bleak at the moment. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4183-->
<!--Post: 4199--><!--Time: 1105848947-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4199"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Boffa994<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Boffa994<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 8:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->hey george give it time. most of the drop offs were found about at least 2 to 3 days after the anniversary. someone probably just hasnt walk on to were its at and found the package he left in a park or somewhere to take to the media or police dpt.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4199-->
